}
\details{
The Simon basket trial design (Cunanan et al. 2017) is an adaptive two-stage
design that aims to efficiently test whether a targeted therapy is effective
across multiple tumor types (baskets) that share a common molecular alteration.

\strong{Design Structure:}
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Stage 1:} Accrue N1 patients across K baskets
\item \strong{Interim Analysis:} Test of homogeneity (Fisher's exact test)
\itemize{
\item If p-value <= gamma: Pursue \strong{heterogeneous path} (analyze separately)
\item If p-value > gamma: Pursue \strong{homogeneous path} (pool all baskets)
}
\item \strong{Stage 2 - Heterogeneous:} For each basket with >= r_s responders,
accrue n2k more patients. Test each continuing basket separately with
Bonferroni correction.
\item \strong{Stage 2 - Homogeneous:} If >= r_c total responders, accrue N2 more
patients pooled. Test overall response rate. Declare all active or all inactive.
}

\strong{Optimization Strategy:}

The function performs grid search over (n2k, gamma, alpha_s, alpha_c) to find
the design that:
\enumerate{
\item Controls FWER <= target_fwer (when A=0)
\item Achieves power >= target_power (when A=calibrate_at_A)
\item Maintains power >= min_power (when A=1)
\item Minimizes a utility function balancing power and sample size
}

\strong{Key Advantages:}
\itemize{
\item More efficient than independent trials when drug works in most/all baskets
\item Simpler than Bayesian approaches
\item Frequentist framework with clear error control
}

\strong{Trade-offs:}
\itemize{
\item May have reduced power when drug works in only one basket
\item Can incorrectly pool active and inactive baskets (specificity decreases as A increases)
\item Only weak FWER control (controlled at A=0 only)
}
}
